tags: scarcity-max, scarcity_max_multiplier

A promise of the node: the most that the price of a resource can be, as a multiple of
its rate. When a resource is scarce, the node can charge more than its rate, but never
more than this multiple.

The value is an integer of 1 or more, in base 10. Example: 3. The value 1 means that
the node never charges more than its rates. It applies to the rates of the resources
cpu, disk and memory.

With no promise, the caller assumes no limit. The node is bound.

